Rowland wrote:

> I really like the chasm idea, and particularly as a Dungeon
> sub-branch. It would add a bit more 'geography' to the dungeon, which
> is a bit 2-dimensional.

I also reckon that the chasm is a nice idea. You could interesting things
with it:

- let monsters walk near the chasm to make the player see how spaces
crumble away and monsters fall down, giving it a feel of urgency
(like on the first quest level with the grudge patch: it was so cool to see
a giant zap a wand of fire at a warrior who subsequently drowned)

- have special sounds and inscriptions/fortunes on/for the chasm level
"you hear hollow screams from the abyss"
"you feel a benumbing hot breeze"

Concerning the instadeath I'm biased towards allowing it (with chances,
as usual; and with probabilities to get saved on the chasm levels further
down):
the most annoying thing (to me) in Nethack is 'you fall into a spiked pit',
'the
spikes are poisoned', 'the poison is deadly'. The chasm would be fairer.



> I know LOTR is heavily used as source reference already, but I'd like
> to see the Balrog restored to some of its literary menace. I've seen
> lots of YAAPs with multiple Balrog kills and an "In your face Gandalf"
> message. Given that when Balrogs usually appear in the game they are
> just another tissue paper monster for high-level players, this is fair
> enough, but trying to deal with one as an XL10-14ish character above
> the castle wand? That would be a challenge: a bit like an early quest
> nemesis, or the titan at Medusa's.
>
> Maybe put the Balrog on a narrow bridge across the chasm. How do you
> deal with it? Lots of choices: fight it, fly round (watch out for the
> bullwhip), jump down (maybe he comes after you), get it off the bridge
> somehow (scare it, confuse it, stagger it), make a hole in the bridge
> (pickaxe, WoDigging, drum of earthquake) and make it fall in then
> cross the hole by jumping... Hopefully enough variety and challenge to
> make it an interesting add to the game?

Another good idea. I think that a lot of monsters (especially demons)
suffer from being generated so late that they are mere cannonfodder (or
at least  interchangeably). The positive aspects of the balrog & bridge
thing IMO are these:
- having to face a difficult monster (if the chasm is rather low in the
dungeon) which _cannot_ summon_ other monsters (is this correct?)
- having the option to retreat and prepare for the fight
- using the pictures known from lore


